---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<BaseLayout title="Workforce Capability Planning System" description="A facility-wide workforce capability system mapping approximately 200 employees across nine departments and roughly 40 skills.">
<section class="case-hero case-capability">
  <div class="container">
    <a class="case-back" href={`${base}/built-systems/`}>← Built Systems</a>
    <div class="case-kicker">04 / Workforce Capability</div>
    <h1>Workforce Capability<br/><em>Planning System</em></h1>
    <p class="case-deck">A facility-wide system for seeing who is trained in what, where coverage is thin, and where cross-training would have the most value.</p>
    <div class="case-role-line"><span>System Design</span><span>Data Modeling</span><span>Requirements Discovery</span><span>Governance</span><span>Workforce Planning</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-metrics case-metrics-light">
  <div class="container case-metric-grid">
    <div><strong>~200</strong><span>Employees Modeled</span></div>
    <div><strong>9</strong><span>Departments</span></div>
    <div><strong>~40</strong><span>Skills / Operations</span></div>
    <div><strong>35</strong><span>Records Reconciled in One Department</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section">
  <div class="container case-two-col">
    <div><div class="case-section-no">01</div><div class="eyebrow">The Visibility Gap</div>
      <h2>Capability existed across the building. Visibility did not.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-copy">
      <p>Training records existed, but they were fragmented across supervisor knowledge and individual competency forms held by HR. Forms had not always been maintained consistently, and records were organized by employee rather than by department, role, or skill.</p>
      <p>When I took over a department with mixed tenure and six to seven different functions, I needed a practical way to see who was trained in what. I built a departmental model first, then expanded the concept after HR identified the same problem at the facility level.</p>
      <p class="case-emphasis">In one department alone, I reconciled 35 competency records, including training completed nearly a decade earlier.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt section-divider">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">02</div><div class="eyebrow">Capability Model</div>
    <h2>Turn scattered records into something people can actually use.</h2>
    <div class="capability-model reveal">
      <div class="cap-node"><small>Employee</small><strong>Person</strong></div><b>→</b>
      <div class="cap-node"><small>Context</small><strong>Department + Tenure</strong></div><b>→</b>
      <div class="cap-node"><small>Capability</small><strong>Skill / Operation</strong></div><b>→</b>
      <div class="cap-node"><small>Depth</small><strong>Competency Level</strong></div><b>→</b>
      <div class="cap-node"><small>Evidence</small><strong>Competency Date</strong></div>
    </div>
    <div class="lookup-pair">
      <article><span>Lookup</span><h3>Employee → Skills</h3><p>See what an employee is trained to do and their current competency level.</p></article>
      <article><span>Reverse Lookup</span><h3>Skill → Employees</h3><p>Find qualified employees when a team needs coverage, backfill, or additional cross-training.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section capability-demo-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">03</div><div class="eyebrow">Portfolio Demo</div>
    <h2>The same system logic, rebuilt in a technology operations context.</h2>
    <p class="case-intro">The original system was developed for a manufacturing workforce. This portfolio version uses entirely fictional technology-industry data so the underlying design can be explored without exposing employer information.</p>

    <div class="cap-demo-window cap-demo-hero">
      <div class="cap-demo-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><b>HR Quick View</b></div>
      <div class="cap-demo-body">
        <div class="cap-demo-title"><small>WORKFORCE CAPABILITY</small><strong>Two-way capability lookup</strong><span>Search from either side of the question.</span></div>
        <div class="cap-demo-kpis">
          <div><span>Employees</span><strong>200</strong></div><div><span>Departments</span><strong>9</strong></div><div><span>Skills</span><strong>40+</strong></div><div><span>Open Actions</span><strong>12</strong></div>
        </div>
        <div class="cap-demo-lookups">
          <div class="cap-lookup-card">
            <label>EMPLOYEE → SKILLS</label><div class="cap-select">Alex Bennett <b>⌄</b></div>
            <div class="cap-table-head"><span>Skill</span><span>Level</span><span>Status</span></div>
            <div class="cap-table-row"><span>Incident Triage</span><span>3</span><em>Qualified</em></div>
            <div class="cap-table-row"><span>Customer Escalation</span><span>3</span><em>Qualified</em></div>
            <div class="cap-table-row"><span>Release Support</span><span>2</span><em>Developing</em></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cap-lookup-card">
            <label>SKILL → EMPLOYEES</label><div class="cap-select">Incident Triage <b>⌄</b></div>
            <div class="cap-table-head"><span>Employee</span><span>Dept.</span><span>Level</span></div>
            <div class="cap-table-row"><span>Jordan Lee</span><span>Support</span><em>3</em></div>
            <div class="cap-table-row"><span>Alex Bennett</span><span>Ops</span><em>3</em></div>
            <div class="cap-table-row"><span>Morgan Chen</span><span>Success</span><em>2</em></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note">Demo labels and names shown here are fictional. The downloadable workbook contains synthetic data only.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt capability-action-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">04</div><div class="eyebrow">From Visibility to Action</div>
    <h2>A matrix is useful. Knowing what needs attention is better.</h2>
    <div class="cap-demo-split">
      <div class="cap-demo-window">
        <div class="cap-demo-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><b>Skill Management</b></div>
        <div class="cap-demo-body compact">
          <div class="cap-demo-title"><small>PRIORITY QUALIFICATION GAPS</small><strong>Coverage risk</strong></div>
          <div class="risk-row risk-high"><span>Critical skill</span><b>1 / 3 qualified</b><em>Critical Gap</em></div>
          <div class="risk-row"><span>Core workflow</span><b>2 / 4 qualified</b><em>Below Target</em></div>
          <div class="risk-row risk-zero"><span>Specialist process</span><b>0 / 2 qualified</b><em>Uncovered</em></div>
          <div class="risk-row"><span>Escalation support</span><b>3 / 4 qualified</b><em>Below Target</em></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cap-action-copy">
        <div><span>01</span><strong>Find single points of failure.</strong><p>Coverage targets make it easier to see where one absence could create a problem.</p></div>
        <div><span>02</span><strong>Prioritize cross-training.</strong><p>Near-ready employees and current coverage help narrow where training would add flexibility.</p></div>
        <div><span>03</span><strong>Keep the decision with the manager.</strong><p>The system surfaces gaps; department leaders still decide what the operation needs next.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section capability-transcript-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">05</div><div class="eyebrow">Individual View</div>
    <h2>One employee record, including cross-department skills.</h2>
    <div class="cap-transcript-wrap">
      <div class="cap-demo-window cap-transcript">
        <div class="cap-demo-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><b>Employee Transcript</b></div>
        <div class="cap-demo-body compact">
          <div class="transcript-head"><div><small>EMPLOYEE</small><strong>Alex Bennett</strong><span>Technology Operations</span></div><div class="transcript-stats"><b>8<small>Current Skills</small></b><b>5<small>Qualified</small></b><b>3<small>Cross-Dept.</small></b></div></div>
          <div class="cap-table-head transcript"><span>Skill / Competency</span><span>Department</span><span>Level</span><span>Readiness</span><span>Cross-Training</span></div>
          <div class="cap-table-row transcript"><span>Incident Triage</span><span>Support</span><span>3</span><em>Qualified</em><span>Cross-Trained</span></div>
          <div class="cap-table-row transcript"><span>Release Support</span><span>Operations</span><span>2</span><em>Developing</em><span>Home Department</span></div>
          <div class="cap-table-row transcript"><span>Customer Escalation</span><span>Success</span><span>3</span><em>Qualified</em><span>Cross-Trained</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Employee-level records make cross-training visible without mixing it into home-department coverage. That keeps the organizational view useful while preserving the full range of skills an employee has developed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt capability-architecture-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">06</div><div class="eyebrow">Operating Model</div>
    <h2>Enter the event once. Use it everywhere else.</h2>
    <div class="cap-architecture">
      <div><small>01</small><strong>Competency Event</strong><span>Approved assessment is recorded</span></div><b>→</b>
      <div><small>02</small><strong>Current Skill Record</strong><span>Matrix and employee record update</span></div><b>→</b>
      <div><small>03</small><strong>Coverage Analysis</strong><span>Lookups and gap views refresh</span></div><b>→</b>
      <div><small>04</small><strong>Training Action</strong><span>Managers can act on the gap</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="cap-supporting"><span>Certifications</span><span>Role Requirements</span><span>Training Plan</span><span>Employee Transcript</span></div>
    <p class="case-note">The demo workbook includes a “How to Use” guide with update triggers, owners, timing, and verification steps so the system can be maintained without relying on its original creator.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section governance-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">07</div><div class="eyebrow">Ownership & Governance</div>
    <h2>Shared ownership keeps the system useful.</h2>
    <div class="governance-equal">
      <div><strong>Supervisors</strong><span>Complete and review competency assessments, identify coverage gaps, and flag cross-training opportunities.</span></div>
      <div><strong>HR</strong><span>Receive completed competency records, enter updates, maintain the central system, and use coverage data for workforce planning.</span></div>
      <div><strong>Leaders / Executives</strong><span>Use capability and coverage data to understand workforce risk, staffing flexibility, and development needs.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note governance-limit"><strong>The main limitation is record upkeep.</strong> If completed training is not documented and entered, the capability view becomes outdated. A future version could connect competency updates directly to training and certification records, reducing the need for manual updates.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-outcomes capability-outcomes">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no light">08</div><div class="eyebrow light">What It Changes</div>
    <h2>From “who knows how to do this?” to a shared workforce view.</h2>
    <div class="outcome-grid">
      <div><strong>2-Way</strong><span>Employee ↔ Skill Lookup</span></div>
      <div><strong>Facility-Wide</strong><span>Coverage Across 9 Departments</span></div>
      <div><strong>Visible</strong><span>Gaps & Single Points of Failure</span></div>
      <div><strong>Transferable</strong><span>Rebuilt for a Different Industry Context</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section capability-download">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="download-card">
      <div><div class="eyebrow">Explore the Demo</div><h2>Technology Workforce Capability System</h2>
      <p>Download the working Excel portfolio demo. All people, departments, skills, and records are fictional; the system design is based on the original workforce capability project.</p></div>
      <a href={`${base}/artifacts/technology-workforce-capability-demo.xlsx`} download><span>Download Excel Demo</span><b>↓</b></a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-next"><div class="container"><span class="eyebrow">Related System</span>
  <a href={`${base}/built-systems/training-certification-system/`}><strong>Training & Certification System</strong><span>→</span></a>
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
